00:01:58.845 --> 00:02:01.650
Have you tried out
that feature yet?

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:02.850
Even smaller group.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:03.480
OK.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:04.720
Well, go check it out.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.287
It's a really exciting feature.

00:02:06.287 --> 00:02:07.620
We launched it a few months ago.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.230
We're really excited about it.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:14.190
But one of the things
we do at Pandora

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:18.960
that's really at sort
of the core of Pandora

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.030
is our personalization.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:24.690
So the idea with
Pandora is that you can

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:26.550
have a lean back experience.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:31.020
You give us some cues, and we
program a continuous stream

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:35.790
of content for you, whether
it's music or non-music content,

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.060
like podcasts or comedy.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.420
So at the heart of
this personalization

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.850
is our analytics system.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:48.060
It's really the
workhorse behind it.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.160
So we have a lot of data.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.960
68 million users in the US.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.260
Those users have created over 13
billion stations over the life

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.550
of Pandora, and 90 billion
pieces of feedback--

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.290
thumbs up or thumbs down--

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.720
telling us what they
like or don't like.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:12.142
And we use all that data
for this personalization.

00:03:12.142 --> 00:03:14.600
So I'll tell you a little bit
about the history of big data

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:15.710
at Pandora.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:18.710
I've actually been
at Pandora 11 years

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:22.880
and was really involved since
the beginning with analytics

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.410
at Pandora.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:28.170
So we launched Pandora in 2005.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:32.180
In 2008, 2009, mobile
really exploded

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:34.830
and the usage of
Pandora really took off.

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:38.030
So originally, we had a
single Postgres database.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:40.880
We were stuffing all of
our analytics data into it,

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.950
and it didn't scale very
well, as you can imagine.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:46.370
So 2010, we started
experimenting

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:50.570
with Hadoop on-prem, and we
built out our first Hadoop

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:54.410
cluster, moved all of our
analytics over to Hadoop.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:55.910
Started with a very
modest cluster--

00:03:55.910 --> 00:04:03.400
10 servers, Apache
0.17 and Hive 0.5.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:07.060
And we've grown
on-prem quite a bit.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.820
So every year, we've had growth.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:14.770
We've added more engineers, more
analysts, more scientists, all

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:16.480
working on a single cluster.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.940
We have one production
cluster that

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:21.850
runs everything on Pandora.

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:24.520
And we've also added a lot
of personalization features.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.480
So thumbprint
radio, if any of you

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.480
use that, personalized
soundtracks, AutoPlay,

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.760
all these personalized
features for our listeners.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:37.930
We've also added features
for our advertisers, as well.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:42.430
Really cool campaign
reporting tools,

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:44.095
some really advanced
forecasting so

00:04:44.095 --> 00:04:46.990
that they can make sure they
have the right campaign,

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:50.260
and some cool segmenting
and targeting, as well.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:53.450
So with all those features,
with all that growth,

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.280
we grew the cluster.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:02.550
So today, we are up to 2,700
datanodes, 7 petabytes of data,

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:06.320
and quite a few
resources on this thing--

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:14.220
so 270 terabytes of
RAM, 76,000 vcores.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:20.580
Just a 10,000-foot overview of
how analytics works at Pandora.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.040
We have the data
ingestion side of things.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:26.490
We leverage Kafka
really heavily.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:29.850
Almost all of our
real-time event data

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:33.090
that happens as you
trigger actions in the app

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:35.280
comes through Kafka now.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.800
We use Sqoop and
some homegrown tools

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:43.770
to load data from our relational
databases into Hadoop.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:50.700
So load all that stuff into
HDFS, leverage Hive and Spark

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:54.300
very heavily for all our
ETL and batch workloads.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.820
Presto and Hive are
the primary tools

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:59.580
that our scientists,
developers, analysts

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:03.210
use as they're
looking for insights.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.730
We also have a couple BI
tools we rely on heavily--

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:09.630
Zeppelin and Tableau
for insights.

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:12.870
And then a large percentage
of what our workload

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:14.970
is on the cluster
are really jobs

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.250
that our development
team, our science team

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:20.970
has built doing
that personalization

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:24.540
and pushing that personalization
that we do typically in a batch

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:28.650
process into production
stores that we

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:31.320
can run our real-time
services off of and make

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.650
these decisions in real time.

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:36.520
So Postgres and
Redis, in particular,

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:38.190
we rely on really heavily.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:47.370
So disaster recovery with our
current on-prem architecture--

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:50.770
we have our main colo,
which is in San Jose.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:52.050
We do have a backup.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:55.650
I would call it more of a
business continuity backup, not

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.520
true disaster recovery.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:01.140
We have a much smaller
cluster, very data-dense,

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:04.500
that's located outside of DC.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:06.270
We have a 40-gigabyte
link that we

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:10.050
copy data to pretty
much all the time

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:14.860
to keep at least a copy
of our data up-to-date.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:17.040
But the big drawback
with this is

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:22.530
if we did need to failover and
rely on our backup data center,

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:24.990
it would take us some time to
scale that out to really take

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:26.320
on the production workload.

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.520
It's just not feasible to run
two clusters the same size.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.820
It would just be sitting
idle all the time.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:35.790
So why cloud?

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.530
You know, we've been
on-prem for a long time.

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:42.482
Why would we kind of pick
up and move to the cloud?

00:07:42.482 --> 00:07:43.940
And that was a big
question for us.

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:51.970
So a lot of different reasons.

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:54.348
Bursty workloads is a
really interesting one.

00:07:54.348 --> 00:07:56.140
Let me just put a pin
in that for a minute.

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:58.480
There's a cool slide I
want to show you next that

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:01.420
gets into that a little bit.

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:05.590
But another avenue
of flexibility

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:06.940
is hardware choices.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:09.610
So we have different workloads.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:11.320
Some of them-- Spark, Presto--

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:12.980
very memory-intensive.

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:17.270
They can do a lot with the
memory if you give it to them.

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:20.120
And all of our
jobs are different.

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:22.060
We have one single
cluster, and we

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:23.900
run a lot of different
types of workloads.

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:27.070
So being able to
switch to a model

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:29.470
where we've separated
storage and compute,

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:32.679
and we can spin up
purpose-driven clusters

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:36.280
with different profiles
for different workloads,

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:39.520
is really appealing to us.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:41.380
Also, multi-regional.

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:44.560
I mentioned the DR
situation for us.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:46.890
Being able to have a
true disaster recovery

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:49.810
is pretty cool.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:53.320
Reduce maintenance-- as the
cluster's grown the last two

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.320
or three years, we've spent
more and more of our time

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:59.680
operationally just keeping
the cluster running for all

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:00.490
of our users--

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:05.260
we've got about 150 users on
the cluster across the company--

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:09.590
and managing the
resources between them.

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:14.950
And we've hit quite a few bugs
just because of our scale.

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:17.890
Things like storage
tiering, and also

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:20.620
understanding costs at
a more granular level,

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:21.610
has been really cool.

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:26.470
So this is another one
on flexibility here.

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:33.323
This is a chart of our Hadoop
utilization over a month.

00:09:33.323 --> 00:09:34.740
And if you look
at this chart, you

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.350
say, OK, well, things
are looking pretty good.

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:43.080
You're under 40%, 35%
across the board as a mean.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:45.810
But because we run
one cluster, we

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:47.610
have a lot of
different users on it,

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:49.410
we're actually
resource-constrained

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:50.890
a lot of the time.

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:52.560
People come in the morning.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:54.060
They want to execute
their queries.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:57.000
They want to learn about what
happened the previous day.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.410
And so we have
bottleneck and backup.

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:02.460
So even though this looks good,
it's really not that efficient

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:06.840
because when people
want to use the cluster,

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:09.490
they can't always get
instant access to it.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:14.160
And so we looked around.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:16.550
We looked at the
different cloud providers.

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:19.970
And something that really
meshed well with Pandora

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:23.990
and what we're trying
to achieve at Pandora--

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:28.118
it was a good fit for
GCP and the Google team.

00:10:28.118 --> 00:10:29.660
And I think the
things that stood out

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:34.040
for us is really the
distributed computing expertise.

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:39.010
Google has a very rich history
with distributed computing.

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:44.270
You know, Hadoop is based on
the original Google File System

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:46.820
and MapReduce papers.

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:52.040
More recently, announcing
the really cool innovations

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:57.110
with Spanner and the kind of
novel way that they handle

00:10:57.110 --> 00:10:59.880
distributed transactions
across the globe.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:01.550
That's pretty exciting, too.

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:06.740
One other thing-- while
this talk and our focus

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:09.440
so far has been on
analytics, one of the things

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:11.900
that's been a challenge
for us at Pandora

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:14.660
is real-time
distributed computing.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:18.470
We built a sharding layer
on top of Postgres way

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:20.420
back in 2011,
because there weren't

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:24.930
a lot of choices for helping
us scale out for real-time data

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:25.430
storage.

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:28.490
So right now we're
focused on analytics,

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:30.410
but we're doing
some investigations

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:34.820
with both multi-regional Spanner
and BigTable as potential data

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:38.060
stores for our
real-time services.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:41.540
So that was a really good
fit, and Google's focus on AI

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:45.220
and machine learning was a
really good match for us.

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:47.570
The investment in
TensorFlow, the ability

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:52.840
to run specialized computing
hardware for graphics,

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:56.780
the TPUs, is pretty exciting.

00:11:56.780 --> 00:12:00.810
In particular, we had a lot of
success with BigQuery, as well,

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:02.717
and we'll go into
more details as we

00:12:02.717 --> 00:12:03.800
get into the meat of this.

00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:09.860
So we kicked off an evaluation.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:13.490
Just to give you kind of
a sense of the timeline,

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:18.110
we're a good size
organization, and this

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:19.150
was a big change for us.

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:22.160
So it took some time to get
buy-in from the business,

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:25.040
to get everybody aligned, and
make sure we're moving forward

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:26.950
in the right direction.

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:28.700
So we kicked off some
preliminary research

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:33.140
into the various cloud
providers at the end of 2017.

00:12:33.140 --> 00:12:37.070
In Q1 2018, we did
a proof of concept,

00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:39.260
spent about three
months building that up.

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:41.820
And by the end of that, from
a technical point of view,

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:43.200
we knew what we wanted to do.

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.830
We knew we wanted to go to GCP.

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:48.590
We spent a couple months working
with a lot of the stakeholders

00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:51.110
around the business,
our partners in finance,

00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:54.170
building out a financial model.

00:12:54.170 --> 00:12:56.180
Something that was
tricky for us is

00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:58.730
that we had this big
investment on-prem.

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:01.730
We had a colo lease that
went out several years.

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:04.770
We had a lot of hardware
we'd already invested in.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:07.760
And so how do we make
the switch to on-prem?

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:10.760
It's not-- or to
GCP and the cloud?

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:12.720
And it's not something
that happens overnight.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:16.100
So it took some
creative work there

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:18.830
to make the numbers work
out, but we got to a point

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:21.950
where we feel like when
we finish this migration,

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:25.670
we're going to be in a good spot
and very comparable, cost-wise,

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:28.550
to where we are today on-prem.

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:33.910
So the end of last year, we
started our migration planning,

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:36.130
trying to figure out
the priority of how

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:40.870
we migrate and move forward.

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:44.140
In Q1, we migrated some
of our initial datasets.

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:45.850
And at the end of
this month, we're

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:48.700
looking to get our first
set of early adopters

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:53.050
onto the GCP platform,
which is pretty exciting.

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:58.470
Still a long way to go,
but making great progress.

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:01.100
So with our POC,
what we did was took

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:02.990
one of our largest datasets.

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:08.600
So this dataset-- it's called
MADDS, Master Advertising

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:10.160
Delivery Data Set.

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:15.170
It's really all of the ad
impressions for all the ads

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:16.490
served on Pandora.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:18.770
It's a very large data
set-- one of our largest.

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:22.610
About 15 billion records in
this data set every month.

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:24.860
We keep a couple years,
actually two or three

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:28.550
years, of this data around
for long-running campaigns

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:29.690
and to do history.

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:37.460
So moved this process up
onto GCP leveraging Dataproc.

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:40.460
It's really a Spark
job that combines

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:43.430
a lot of different data
sources into one data set,

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:50.210
normalizes that data,
and abstracts away a lot

00:14:50.210 --> 00:14:53.210
of the business logic,
that downstream consumers

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:55.020
of this data--

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:58.250
so they don't really
need to know about that.

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:01.890
So this all went really well.

00:15:01.890 --> 00:15:06.070
We found increased query
performance with BigQuery.

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:08.720
I'll get you some
numbers in a minute.

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:12.320
The batch workload
was also optimized.

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:17.730
About 35% fewer resources to
do the same amount of work.

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:20.340
And then this
consolidation I mentioned.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:23.030
We were able to move
all of these sources

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:24.290
into a singular table.

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:30.590
So this is my favorite slide.

00:15:30.590 --> 00:15:32.930
What we did was we took
the gnarliest queries

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:35.870
that we ran on-prem
on this dataset,

00:15:35.870 --> 00:15:37.910
and we ran them
against BigQuery,

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:41.090
ran them against our
Presto instance on-prem,

00:15:41.090 --> 00:15:43.730
against our Hive on-prem.

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:49.290
And I think not only
the quick results here,

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:54.510
but the consistency across
all, under 30 seconds here.

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:56.860
Very reliable.

00:15:56.860 --> 00:15:59.130
So this was really,
really exciting for us.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:09.280
So as we talk about
the migration,

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:10.430
this is a long process.

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:12.970
We have a lot of jobs running
in the cluster, a lot of users

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:14.890
on the cluster.

00:16:14.890 --> 00:16:18.130
We think this is about
an 18-month effort.

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:19.690
And we're part of the way in.

00:16:19.690 --> 00:16:21.940
You know, originally
we did a pilot.

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:23.560
We moved a couple
of datasets up,

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:25.970
different types,
got some experience,

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:28.600
did a lot of the
infrastructure work.

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:30.070
Cloud is new for us.

00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:32.050
I'll talk about
that in a minute.

00:16:32.050 --> 00:16:33.790
But it's a different
animal, and there

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:35.560
was a lot of learning there.

00:16:35.560 --> 00:16:37.930
Now we're deep into
the core datasets,

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:41.260
migrating the datasets
that are most heavily used

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:43.837
by the company up there.

00:16:43.837 --> 00:16:44.420
We'll do that.

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:46.960
And then there's going to
be some long tail, and also

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:47.770
an opportunity.

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:49.870
We've built some
tools that tell us

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:52.610
really what's
being used on-prem,

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:54.170
how often it's being used.

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:57.160
And so to do that, we've
been able to prioritize

00:16:57.160 --> 00:16:59.415
the workloads that
we're moving over,

00:16:59.415 --> 00:17:01.540
and I think we'll do some
spring cleaning, as well.

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:08.050
So areas of focus.

00:17:08.050 --> 00:17:12.119
I mentioned we spent a lot of
time on the infrastructure one

00:17:12.119 --> 00:17:15.530
to get right, and it
was a new muscle for us.

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:21.890
So we leverage
Terraform very heavily.

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:24.950
We want everything to
be really repeatable.

00:17:24.950 --> 00:17:31.950
Between our operations
team and our tech ops team,

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:34.190
they built essentially
a project creation tool.

00:17:34.190 --> 00:17:39.230
So someone that wants to go and
use the GCP platform at Pandora

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:42.890
now goes to a portal,
gives us some information,

00:17:42.890 --> 00:17:48.620
gives us some contact and
relevant information about what

00:17:48.620 --> 00:17:50.570
this project is
going to be used for,

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:53.510
and that kicks off some
Terraform that goes and creates

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:58.880
the project, gives them the
right set of permissions,

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:02.210
and does things in a standard
and reproducible way.

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:05.620
So that was really cool.

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:08.870
VPCs were another thing took
us a while to get right.

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:12.170
We worked a lot with
the Google PSO group

00:18:12.170 --> 00:18:13.940
and some of the product teams.

00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:15.620
Learned a lot there.

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:17.370
But also automated
that, as well,

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:20.060
so the VPCs get attached
to these projects

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:22.310
as they get created.

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:24.830
Also, important thing
for us was budgeting,

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:29.090
so being able to map these
projects to different budgets,

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:32.070
and understand what
that use case was going

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:37.280
to be because even now, over
just our short time on GCP,

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:42.950
I think we're at, I don't know,
nearing 100 projects or so.

00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:45.110
And then of course,
the users are

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:48.050
going to be an important track.

00:18:48.050 --> 00:18:49.250
It's a switch.

00:18:49.250 --> 00:18:50.060
We use Hive.

00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:52.010
We use Presto on-prem.

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:54.560
BigQuery is still SQL, but
there's some nuance there.

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:58.130
There's some UDFs that
we'll be transforming,

00:18:58.130 --> 00:19:01.950
and it's going to be
a learning process.

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:05.280
We think we're going to do
this in a few different tracks.

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:07.980
We'll have some training for
different types of users.

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:11.430
We've got the administrators
that have been kind of trained

00:19:11.430 --> 00:19:12.450
up already.

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:14.490
Different tracks for a
developer, scientist,

00:19:14.490 --> 00:19:17.025
and analyst to learn
about the platform.

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:22.450
So this is really cool.

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:26.500
Very early on, we figured
out that we actually have

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:29.270
a lot of cruft on our system.

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:31.580
We've been around
for a long time.

00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:33.220
We want to make
sure we're spending

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:37.970
our time moving the things
that are most important.

00:19:37.970 --> 00:19:40.570
So shout-out to one of our
engineers who's in the audience

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:41.950
here, Kate Shimada.

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:45.490
She went and created what
we call the lineage tool,

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:49.680
so scraped all the logs
from our on-prem cluster.

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:53.690
Think timeline server logs for
TES, job history for MapReduce,

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:57.160
some logs for Presto
and from Airflow.

00:19:57.160 --> 00:19:59.650
Kind of pulled all that
data together, figured out

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:02.590
the relationships, parsed
the actual queries that

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:05.860
are being run, and
figured out things like,

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:08.470
what are the top queried tables?

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:14.260
Dependencies between datasets--
what are two commonly paired

00:20:14.260 --> 00:20:16.870
tables that people
join on together when

00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:18.100
they're running queries?

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:19.970
And then what are the
least queried tables?

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:23.190
What are things that just
aren't really being used?

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:27.970
And those are prime
candidates to be axed and do

00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:28.930
a little cleanup.

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:35.370
So as we get into
migration, we took the data

00:20:35.370 --> 00:20:37.590
from the lineage
tool, broke it up

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:41.520
into functional areas of
work, assigned leaders

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:44.220
to the different areas
of work, and then we're

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:46.920
actually also
using the same tool

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:51.390
to check off datasets and
jobs as they get migrated

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:52.695
and move over to GCP.

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:56.710
All right.

00:20:56.710 --> 00:20:58.168
I'm going to hand
it over to Blake,

00:20:58.168 --> 00:21:00.400
and he's going to go
a little more deep

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:02.469
into the technical details.

00:21:02.469 --> 00:21:03.636
BLAKE DUBOIS: Thanks, Brett.

00:21:03.636 --> 00:21:07.094
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:11.540 --> 00:21:15.350
So I just first want to call
out the team in the timeline.

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:18.160
So first off, Google
PSO engaged with Pandora

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:21.550
in around November of last year,
along with a wonderful partner.

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:24.200
Some of those members are in
the United States, Neudesic,

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:25.840
so this was really
a combined effort

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:29.350
between Pandora's excellent
engineering organization,

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:31.270
Neudesic, and Google PSO.

00:21:31.270 --> 00:21:34.510
And we're going to tell
you a little bit about what

00:21:34.510 --> 00:21:38.390
the architecture
looks like in GCP.

00:21:38.390 --> 00:21:41.330
So one of the first
things we did is target--

00:21:41.330 --> 00:21:43.853
Pandora has a large,
monolithic cluster on-premises,

00:21:43.853 --> 00:21:45.770
and Brett did a really
good job of showing you

00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:48.312
some of the inefficiencies, and
some of the bursty workloads,

00:21:48.312 --> 00:21:51.900
and some of the drivers that
pushed them to the cloud.

00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:55.270
And so they were also facing
some operational burdens.

00:21:55.270 --> 00:21:57.800
And so they really
chose GCP Dataproc,

00:21:57.800 --> 00:22:02.270
which is GCP's completely
managed Hadoop platform.

00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:06.230
And we really run Dataproc
in two different ways.

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:08.270
So on the left-hand side,
we see that we spin up

00:22:08.270 --> 00:22:11.570
Dataproc job, Dataproc
clusters, in around 90 seconds

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:13.860
or so that are scoped
to particular jobs.

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:16.460
So this is ideal
for batch or ETL

00:22:16.460 --> 00:22:19.280
processes that get
run on a daily basis,

00:22:19.280 --> 00:22:20.850
if not more frequently.

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:23.300
And so what we see
here is that we're

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:26.720
using Apache Airflow, in the
form of GCP Composer, a managed

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:30.470
version of Apache Airflow,
to basically identify

00:22:30.470 --> 00:22:32.420
how much data needs to
be processed, spin up

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:36.240
a right-sized Dataproc cluster,
do the ETL or batch process,

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:39.080
and then persist the result
back to Google Cloud Storage

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:41.840
or push it into BigQuery.

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:44.030
On the right-hand side,
there are some use cases

00:22:44.030 --> 00:22:46.460
that Pandora undertakes, like
the use of Hive or Presto

00:22:46.460 --> 00:22:49.550
for interactive query, to
support 150 or so users

00:22:49.550 --> 00:22:50.990
throughout the day.

00:22:50.990 --> 00:22:55.820
And these are served best
by long living or moderately

00:22:55.820 --> 00:22:58.130
living Dataproc clusters.

00:22:58.130 --> 00:23:00.320
These clusters can
actually be spun up

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:02.840
in an optimized
fashion per workload.

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:05.900
For example, Hive may need a
more balance between compute

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:07.490
and memory, while
Presto would have

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:08.782
more memory-intensive machines.

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:14.180
It also allows the Pandora
team to speed up new clusters,

00:23:14.180 --> 00:23:16.135
test out new
versions of software

00:23:16.135 --> 00:23:20.630
via new Dataproc
images, to see if they

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:24.170
can upgrade to a new version
of Hive or Spark, et cetera.

00:23:24.170 --> 00:23:25.670
On the edges of
some of these things

00:23:25.670 --> 00:23:28.730
are some operational tools
that are used on a daily basis.

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:30.980
So these include things like
Google Stackdriver, which

00:23:30.980 --> 00:23:34.280
has direct integration
with GCP Dataproc

00:23:34.280 --> 00:23:38.390
to pull out a lot of YARN
metrics, amongst other things,

00:23:38.390 --> 00:23:42.050
as well as like Google
Cloud Logging for IEM,

00:23:42.050 --> 00:23:43.730
and other metrics.

00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:45.430
And then lastly,
on the edge nodes,

00:23:45.430 --> 00:23:48.920
clients can use
edge nodes either

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:52.280
in the cloud to spin up
Jupiter or Zeppelin notebooks,

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:56.510
or they can also access
across their interconnect

00:23:56.510 --> 00:23:57.830
from their on-premises systems.

00:24:01.130 --> 00:24:05.260
So now I'm going to walk
through all the pillars

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:07.540
of a successful data
platform on GCP,

00:24:07.540 --> 00:24:10.450
and what are some of the
things that Pandora--

00:24:10.450 --> 00:24:12.020
some key decisions
they had to make,

00:24:12.020 --> 00:24:14.227
and what steps that
they had to take.

00:24:14.227 --> 00:24:16.060
So the first thing we're
going to talk about

00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:17.380
is data ingestion.

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:22.360
So Pandora has over 7 petabytes
of data on their Hadoop cluster

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:24.040
on-premises today,
and they needed

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:26.950
to devise a plan to
get that into GCP.

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:28.670
So the first thing
is connectivity.

00:24:28.670 --> 00:24:32.020
We typically, when we
work with customers,

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:36.820
look to see which pattern
is right for them--

00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:38.410
either moving it
across the wire,

00:24:38.410 --> 00:24:41.200
or using some type of
transfer appliance.

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:43.900
In this case, Pandora, when
we started working with them,

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:45.820
had a 10-gig link.

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:47.602
That was to GCP.

00:24:47.602 --> 00:24:49.060
And over the course
of the project,

00:24:49.060 --> 00:24:50.987
that was increased
to 40 gigabit.

00:24:50.987 --> 00:24:52.570
We also learned
throughout the project

00:24:52.570 --> 00:24:55.630
that, like Brett mentioned
earlier with his comments

00:24:55.630 --> 00:24:57.640
on VPC, network design is key.

00:24:57.640 --> 00:25:00.250
So working with
infrastructure folks

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:03.190
in the beginning to make sure
that you have that foundation

00:25:03.190 --> 00:25:05.080
is very, very important.

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:07.870
Next, now that we had
connectivity established,

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:11.628
we had to go, how are we
going to get data into GCP?

00:25:11.628 --> 00:25:13.670
How are we going to get
into Google Cloud Storage

00:25:13.670 --> 00:25:16.700
to realize that separation
of compute and storage?

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:18.640
So there's really three
different patterns

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:20.050
for doing this.

00:25:20.050 --> 00:25:22.110
And for those that
are interested,

00:25:22.110 --> 00:25:25.150
these slides will be
posted in PDF form,

00:25:25.150 --> 00:25:27.170
and this is actually a link.

00:25:27.170 --> 00:25:28.950
So there's push, versus
pull, versus kind

00:25:28.950 --> 00:25:30.650
of having a proxy cluster.

00:25:30.650 --> 00:25:33.760
So I'm going to focus
on push versus pull.

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:38.490
The push model is, we have
a Hadoop cluster on-premises

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:41.500
that you install a GCS
connector JAR into,

00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:43.480
and you point it at
Google Cloud Storage

00:25:43.480 --> 00:25:45.760
and move the data over time.

00:25:45.760 --> 00:25:49.510
The other scenario is you can
spin up a Google Cloud Dataproc

00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:52.900
cluster in GCP, point it at
your on-premises cluster,

00:25:52.900 --> 00:25:54.320
and pull the data.

00:25:54.320 --> 00:25:56.450
There are pluses and
minuses to each approach,

00:25:56.450 --> 00:25:59.120
and you can read
more on the link.

00:25:59.120 --> 00:26:01.700
So Pandora decided to
do the push model-- use

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:04.360
their existing cluster
to push data into GCP,

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:06.280
one of the easier options.

00:26:06.280 --> 00:26:09.970
But to do this, they kind
of ran into an issue.

00:26:09.970 --> 00:26:12.760
There is a mismatch
between checksums

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:17.193
between Hadoop historically
and Google Cloud Storage,

00:26:17.193 --> 00:26:18.610
and not just Google
Cloud Storage,

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:21.640
but object stores in
the cloud in general.

00:26:21.640 --> 00:26:24.310
At a high level, this has to
do with a mismatch between MD5

00:26:24.310 --> 00:26:26.110
and CRC checksums.

00:26:26.110 --> 00:26:28.360
So one of the senior staff
engineers within the Google

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:33.730
Cloud Dataproc team developed
a patch, HDFS-13056,

00:26:33.730 --> 00:26:35.770
which is a patch to HDFS.

00:26:35.770 --> 00:26:37.690
So the Google PSO
team, Neudesic,

00:26:37.690 --> 00:26:39.670
and Pandora collaborated
on this patch,

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:44.830
and applied this to Pandora's
on-premises cluster through

00:26:44.830 --> 00:26:48.160
a dev, test, and finally a
rollout to their 2,700-node

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:49.790
production cluster.

00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:53.170
So this really allowed them to
move the data from on-premises

00:26:53.170 --> 00:26:56.470
to the cloud, have assurances
that the integrity of the data

00:26:56.470 --> 00:26:59.290
was preserved, so that they
can start deleting datasets

00:26:59.290 --> 00:27:03.580
from on-premises, and
that has just started.

00:27:03.580 --> 00:27:05.550
Next is when dealing
with data copy

00:27:05.550 --> 00:27:07.300
when moving from
on-premises to the cloud,

00:27:07.300 --> 00:27:10.655
you have to be cognizant
of data access.

00:27:10.655 --> 00:27:12.280
And so what I primarily
mean about that

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:16.150
is the cost of ingress and
egress from Google Cloud

00:27:16.150 --> 00:27:17.710
and cloud in general.

00:27:17.710 --> 00:27:18.730
The first is ingress.

00:27:18.730 --> 00:27:21.370
So as we moved the
7 petabytes of data

00:27:21.370 --> 00:27:23.087
from on-premises
to the cloud, we

00:27:23.087 --> 00:27:24.670
don't have to worry
about that charge.

00:27:24.670 --> 00:27:26.410
It is completely free.

00:27:26.410 --> 00:27:29.857
But we do have to be
cognizant of egress charges,

00:27:29.857 --> 00:27:31.190
and they come in two main forms.

00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:33.560
One is across zones
in a particular region

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:35.955
and when moving between regions.

00:27:35.955 --> 00:27:37.330
This is especially
important when

00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:39.038
dealing with disaster
recovery, and we're

00:27:39.038 --> 00:27:41.390
going to talk a little
bit about that later.

00:27:41.390 --> 00:27:46.830
But lastly, due to Pandora's
major investment in BigQuery,

00:27:46.830 --> 00:27:50.140
it really softens a lot of that.

00:27:50.140 --> 00:27:53.300
So any query egress from
BigQuery is completely free.

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:56.620
So when you're serving
analytical applications or BI

00:27:56.620 --> 00:27:59.440
tools, either in the
cloud or back on-premises,

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:02.325
any egress from
that engine is free.

00:28:02.325 --> 00:28:04.700
And the last thing I just
wanted to share about the slide

00:28:04.700 --> 00:28:06.440
is that there's a
great, excellent blog

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:09.500
post that goes into the
details between the HDFS patch

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:12.770
that is required to
ensure data integrity

00:28:12.770 --> 00:28:15.020
between on-premises Hadoop
clusters and the cloud,

00:28:15.020 --> 00:28:17.600
and you can read more by
clicking the blog post link.

00:28:19.958 --> 00:28:22.500
Next thing I want to do is talk
a little about cloud storage.

00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:24.167
So we're moving data
into cloud storage.

00:28:28.985 --> 00:28:31.880
In an on-premises Hadoop
cluster with HDFS,

00:28:31.880 --> 00:28:35.570
there's not the nuanced
durability, availability,

00:28:35.570 --> 00:28:38.040
resiliency in the storage.

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:39.710
There is a concept
of storage tiering,

00:28:39.710 --> 00:28:44.090
but there isn't quite the
same analog into the cloud.

00:28:44.090 --> 00:28:46.370
So the first things
that we had to deal with

00:28:46.370 --> 00:28:49.470
were storage classes.

00:28:49.470 --> 00:28:51.900
So Pandora worked
with the PSO team

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:55.113
to understand what was
right for them, based

00:28:55.113 --> 00:28:56.780
on the different
datasets that they had.

00:28:56.780 --> 00:28:59.540
So datasets come in
a few major flavors,

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:03.500
so gold master datasets
that need to be highly

00:29:03.500 --> 00:29:06.135
available at a low latency.

00:29:06.135 --> 00:29:07.760
They need to be backed
up and available

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:09.830
to any type of DR system.

00:29:09.830 --> 00:29:12.740
There's data that's created
due to intermediate processes

00:29:12.740 --> 00:29:14.780
of analytical jobs
that can be rerun.

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:18.830
And that data requires
less resiliency, but high

00:29:18.830 --> 00:29:19.740
performance.

00:29:19.740 --> 00:29:21.710
And lastly, there's
just intermediate data

00:29:21.710 --> 00:29:24.410
that analysts use throughout
the data science process,

00:29:24.410 --> 00:29:28.820
and that may not need high
resiliency, durability,

00:29:28.820 --> 00:29:31.710
or performance.

00:29:31.710 --> 00:29:35.250
And so what you see here
is an engineer at Pandora.

00:29:35.250 --> 00:29:37.700
Iggy really empowered
his organization

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:40.580
to understand all these storage
classes via the decision tree

00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:43.270
on the right.

00:29:43.270 --> 00:29:45.280
And then next, moving
on, is permissions.

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:49.530
So a lot of times
I go and I work

00:29:49.530 --> 00:29:52.050
with customers who are
storing a lot of data in HDFS,

00:29:52.050 --> 00:29:54.540
and it's kind of amazing
to see, although this

00:29:54.540 --> 00:29:57.330
isn't the case of Pandora,
that the majority of jobs

00:29:57.330 --> 00:29:59.790
are accessed and a
majority of files

00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:02.190
are owned by the Hadoop user.

00:30:02.190 --> 00:30:07.230
And so when moving to the cloud,
having to understand IAM--

00:30:07.230 --> 00:30:09.390
Identity and Access
Management-- service accounts,

00:30:09.390 --> 00:30:11.430
and principles is key.

00:30:11.430 --> 00:30:15.510
So we were able to implement
a project-based inherited

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:20.130
security model with projects
being scoped to applications

00:30:20.130 --> 00:30:22.860
and projects being available
for dev test and production

00:30:22.860 --> 00:30:27.060
to encapsulate the security
of the actual application

00:30:27.060 --> 00:30:29.828
and its data.

00:30:29.828 --> 00:30:32.120
So this goes without saying,
but separating the compute

00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:36.570
and the storage really alleviate
a lot of pain customers

00:30:36.570 --> 00:30:39.150
normally experience,
as did Pandora,

00:30:39.150 --> 00:30:41.865
that when HDFS gets
above 90% or so capacity,

00:30:41.865 --> 00:30:44.550
that it can bring the entire
cluster to a grinding halt.

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:48.330
So that is a problem that is
completely alleviated by moving

00:30:48.330 --> 00:30:52.110
to GCP and GCS.

00:30:52.110 --> 00:30:53.970
It also increases transparency.

00:30:53.970 --> 00:30:58.500
So now that you
have data up in GCS,

00:30:58.500 --> 00:31:01.880
you can really point any type
of analytical engine at it,

00:31:01.880 --> 00:31:04.560
and it's very, very easy.

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:07.570
And then lastly, we talked about
where we are in this process.

00:31:07.570 --> 00:31:09.270
It's very early
days for Pandora.

00:31:09.270 --> 00:31:12.240
But some of the discussions
that are already ongoing

00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:15.060
are around object
lifecycle, which usually

00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:17.430
requires custom tooling.

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:19.710
Certain scenarios that
are covered under this

00:31:19.710 --> 00:31:23.790
include what happens when a
data scientist or an analyst

00:31:23.790 --> 00:31:26.835
takes a gold master dataset,
runs a bunch of analysis on it,

00:31:26.835 --> 00:31:28.710
makes a lot of analysis
result, and it's just

00:31:28.710 --> 00:31:30.870
sitting in a GCS bucket?

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:33.810
Now that you have access
to unlimited storage,

00:31:33.810 --> 00:31:36.990
you may start seeing a little
bit more cruft laying around.

00:31:36.990 --> 00:31:38.485
So how do you manage that?

00:31:38.485 --> 00:31:40.610
How do you ensure that the
permissions are correct?

00:31:40.610 --> 00:31:43.290
How do you ensure
that datasets are

00:31:43.290 --> 00:31:45.958
known to be either
intermediary or gold?

00:31:45.958 --> 00:31:46.750
So on and so forth.

00:31:46.750 --> 00:31:51.120
And so while manual
processes may work at first,

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:54.480
it's very common to build
custom tooling to automate this.

00:31:57.240 --> 00:32:01.800
So here's the real meat
of the data platform

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:04.440
today using Cloud Dataproc.

00:32:04.440 --> 00:32:07.620
So there's a few different
ways of using Cloud Dataproc,

00:32:07.620 --> 00:32:09.390
and I've tried to
break them down here

00:32:09.390 --> 00:32:10.800
in four different ways.

00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:13.530
So we talked about
ephemeral versus

00:32:13.530 --> 00:32:15.810
long-living Dataproc clusters.

00:32:15.810 --> 00:32:21.038
And so Pandora basically settled
on the four following methods

00:32:21.038 --> 00:32:21.830
for using Dataproc.

00:32:21.830 --> 00:32:27.120
So the first is long-running
Dataproc clusters

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:29.560
that run workloads
like Presto or Hive,

00:32:29.560 --> 00:32:31.080
but are capable of auto-scaling.

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:33.940
So when users are not
using these clusters,

00:32:33.940 --> 00:32:36.190
they can scale down,
save cost, et cetera.

00:32:36.190 --> 00:32:38.880
But whenever people come in in
the morning and start firing

00:32:38.880 --> 00:32:42.390
queries, that they
can auto-scale.

00:32:42.390 --> 00:32:45.050
The next is
long-running Spark jobs.

00:32:45.050 --> 00:32:49.890
The workload is
streaming workloads.

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:53.670
These take advantage
of auto-scaling,

00:32:53.670 --> 00:32:58.320
but may not scale back down
due to some of the concerns

00:32:58.320 --> 00:33:00.300
around intermediate data.

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:03.180
And then the next two are
around ephemeral clusters.

00:33:03.180 --> 00:33:05.760
So ephemeral clusters,
or job-scoped clusters,

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:08.040
can be brought up to do ETL.

00:33:08.040 --> 00:33:12.810
They can be created
in 90 seconds.

00:33:12.810 --> 00:33:15.450
We can create a cluster
that has 500 nodes that

00:33:15.450 --> 00:33:17.160
will run for a period
of minutes, which

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:21.870
would cost the same as a
one-node cluster running

00:33:21.870 --> 00:33:24.720
for 500 minutes,
and just really see

00:33:24.720 --> 00:33:27.450
that immense
speed-up in the cycle

00:33:27.450 --> 00:33:30.070
time for the particular job.

00:33:30.070 --> 00:33:32.700
And while these don't
necessarily auto-scale today,

00:33:32.700 --> 00:33:34.090
they can be dynamic at startup.

00:33:34.090 --> 00:33:37.410
So when you're using Apache
Airflow or Composer to create

00:33:37.410 --> 00:33:40.270
these clusters, you can identify
how much work is being done,

00:33:40.270 --> 00:33:42.240
and pick the amount
of nodes that should

00:33:42.240 --> 00:33:46.110
be used to perform this job.

00:33:46.110 --> 00:33:48.060
And then lastly is
ephemeral clusters.

00:33:48.060 --> 00:33:54.420
So jobs that may need to copy
data from one place to another,

00:33:54.420 --> 00:33:56.760
or perform some
type of operation,

00:33:56.760 --> 00:34:01.260
like analyze tables in Hive,
may be created a ephemerally

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:06.230
to spin up and spin back down
in a short period of time.

00:34:06.230 --> 00:34:07.906
So there's a few
things on the right

00:34:07.906 --> 00:34:09.989
that I want to talk about,
like over the last five

00:34:09.989 --> 00:34:12.570
months that we were able to
realize pretty easily by using

00:34:12.570 --> 00:34:14.020
a managed Hadoop service.

00:34:14.020 --> 00:34:16.739
So the first thing
is Pandora was

00:34:16.739 --> 00:34:18.810
able to upgrade to Hadoop 2.9.

00:34:18.810 --> 00:34:22.409
So you saw that they were
running HDP 2.6.4, which

00:34:22.409 --> 00:34:29.429
was Hadoop 2.7.2, and it had a
lower version of Hive, as well.

00:34:29.429 --> 00:34:32.520
So with very little
effort, we were

00:34:32.520 --> 00:34:37.830
able to adopt a Dataproc image
with Hadoop 2.9 and Hive 2.3

00:34:37.830 --> 00:34:40.889
and realize some
performance gains.

00:34:40.889 --> 00:34:44.610
The next is I want to talk a
little bit about customization

00:34:44.610 --> 00:34:45.420
of Dataproc.

00:34:45.420 --> 00:34:48.239
So Dataproc has base
images, but they rarely

00:34:48.239 --> 00:34:52.181
have every single component that
a customer may want to utilize.

00:34:52.181 --> 00:34:53.639
And that was the
case with Pandora.

00:34:53.639 --> 00:34:58.140
So Dataproc offers a series
of top-level components

00:34:58.140 --> 00:35:00.930
that are baked into the
image that are enabled

00:35:00.930 --> 00:35:02.850
or disabled based on flags.

00:35:02.850 --> 00:35:05.010
And so what this means is
since the bit's already

00:35:05.010 --> 00:35:08.340
built into the
image, you can have

00:35:08.340 --> 00:35:11.250
a litany of components available
within the 90-second cluster

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:12.390
startup time.

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:15.420
And so what this
means is that you just

00:35:15.420 --> 00:35:17.640
write in an
initialization action that

00:35:17.640 --> 00:35:20.610
has configuration and
not necessarily bits that

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:23.100
need to be installed, because
the sum of installing all

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:26.760
those bits across a
cluster, even a particularly

00:35:26.760 --> 00:35:29.230
large cluster, could
be very costly,

00:35:29.230 --> 00:35:31.830
both in performance
as well as cost.

00:35:31.830 --> 00:35:34.530
And then lastly, we talked a
little bit about auto-scaling

00:35:34.530 --> 00:35:37.470
for Dataproc, but I do want to
talk about workflow templates.

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:40.560
So workflow templates are a
recent innovation in Dataproc

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:43.210
that really allows you
to spin up a cluster,

00:35:43.210 --> 00:35:47.640
run a graph of jobs, and then
let the cluster spin back down.

00:35:47.640 --> 00:35:51.790
So this really,
really simplifies

00:35:51.790 --> 00:35:55.260
the job-scoped ephemeral
cluster model where you once

00:35:55.260 --> 00:35:58.620
had to create a Dataproc
job, submit jobs, and then

00:35:58.620 --> 00:36:00.520
understand when to
shut down your cluster.

00:36:00.520 --> 00:36:02.640
This is now completely
wrapped for you,

00:36:02.640 --> 00:36:05.520
and easily called by scheduling
systems like Airflow.

00:36:09.060 --> 00:36:11.250
Next, I want to talk a
little about Cloud Composer.

00:36:11.250 --> 00:36:17.430
So Pandora is a very big
investor in Apache Airflow.

00:36:17.430 --> 00:36:19.800
They primarily run
Airflow 1.8 on-premises,

00:36:19.800 --> 00:36:22.200
and have over 90
instances today.

00:36:22.200 --> 00:36:26.700
These instances span the gamut
of dev, test, and production.

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:28.980
They have an extensive
set of plugins

00:36:28.980 --> 00:36:31.540
that they use to do
some really cool stuff.

00:36:31.540 --> 00:36:33.530
One of them is job dependencies.

00:36:33.530 --> 00:36:35.820
So not only do
they have a series

00:36:35.820 --> 00:36:38.280
of plugins that will manage
job dependencies across Airflow

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:42.480
instances, but they also manage
dependencies across legacy

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:45.300
schedulers, and now recently,
they've added functionality

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:48.120
to also mention dependencies
across the cloud, as

00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:53.010
well, which is GCP Composer,
which is managed Airflow.

00:36:53.010 --> 00:36:55.440
They also have a lot
of cool operators

00:36:55.440 --> 00:36:59.430
that do things like sense
whether data is in HDFS, sense

00:36:59.430 --> 00:37:01.110
whether or not a
partition in Hive

00:37:01.110 --> 00:37:06.780
is available to
basically enable jobs

00:37:06.780 --> 00:37:09.690
to understand if they can move
down their dependency graph.

00:37:11.953 --> 00:37:13.370
Continuing along
with the plugins,

00:37:13.370 --> 00:37:17.660
there's also CI/CD built
into the Airflow instances.

00:37:17.660 --> 00:37:20.600
Since Airflow is so
pervasive at Pandora,

00:37:20.600 --> 00:37:23.120
it shouldn't come as a shock
that it's not just engineers

00:37:23.120 --> 00:37:23.870
that use Airflow.

00:37:23.870 --> 00:37:25.710
It's a lot of analysts
and scientists, too.

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:29.672
So how can you enable these
people to quickly write a DAG

00:37:29.672 --> 00:37:31.130
and sync the updates
to the system?

00:37:31.130 --> 00:37:35.360
So they have a update
DAG view plug-in

00:37:35.360 --> 00:37:37.910
that works across Airflow
and Composer in GCP.

00:37:40.550 --> 00:37:42.800
Last, when we
started this project,

00:37:42.800 --> 00:37:48.470
upgrades to Composer in GCP were
a little crufty, to be honest.

00:37:48.470 --> 00:37:51.200
So there was custom
scripting that

00:37:51.200 --> 00:37:55.610
was required to upgrade minor
major versions of both Composer

00:37:55.610 --> 00:37:59.000
and it's underlying Airflow.

00:37:59.000 --> 00:38:02.990
And we're really happy to
report that as of Next, I

00:38:02.990 --> 00:38:06.680
believe yesterday, new
functionality was introduced

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:10.910
in Composer that completely
automates both tools upgrade

00:38:10.910 --> 00:38:12.170
automatically.

00:38:12.170 --> 00:38:15.168
And then lastly, I just want
to talk through this system

00:38:15.168 --> 00:38:15.710
on the right.

00:38:15.710 --> 00:38:19.340
So the partner that I called out
in the beginning of this talk

00:38:19.340 --> 00:38:22.640
really took the lead in
developing a Composer DAG

00:38:22.640 --> 00:38:23.450
factory.

00:38:23.450 --> 00:38:26.150
And so what this
is is a framework

00:38:26.150 --> 00:38:29.780
that runs in Composer that
encapsulates all the jobs that

00:38:29.780 --> 00:38:33.540
are being migrated to GCP
from Pandora's on-premises.

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:36.950
And so it really just wraps
analytical business logic

00:38:36.950 --> 00:38:43.740
in a lot of prechecks, sensor
checks, task retries, logging,

00:38:43.740 --> 00:38:47.130
et cetera, validation,
and then also

00:38:47.130 --> 00:38:52.600
queues other processes for
downstream processing, as well.

00:38:52.600 --> 00:38:58.410
And so this DAG factory is being
used to wrap all of the jobs

00:38:58.410 --> 00:39:01.372
are being moved to
GCP from on-premises.

00:39:04.610 --> 00:39:06.890
If you want to learn
a lot more about this,

00:39:06.890 --> 00:39:08.570
you can catch this
great session that's

00:39:08.570 --> 00:39:10.100
taking place tomorrow morning.

00:39:10.100 --> 00:39:12.350
So Dan Barrett, a staff
software engineer from Pandora

00:39:12.350 --> 00:39:13.370
who's in the
audience here today--

00:39:13.370 --> 00:39:15.162
he's going to be speaking
with the Composer

00:39:15.162 --> 00:39:19.700
team about Pandora's
move to GCP and Composer.

00:39:19.700 --> 00:39:22.970
That's tomorrow morning
at the Metreon Theater 7.

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:28.800
So next is BigQuery.

00:39:28.800 --> 00:39:32.550
So Pandora has recently been
experimenting with BigQuery,

00:39:32.550 --> 00:39:38.472
and is settling on a production
architecture for its use.

00:39:38.472 --> 00:39:40.680
And so I'm going to step
through a couple of concerns

00:39:40.680 --> 00:39:44.820
that customers typically
have to understand

00:39:44.820 --> 00:39:45.870
when they adopt BigQuery.

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:55.790
So the first is
workload management.

00:39:55.790 --> 00:39:58.330
So first off, before we go
into to BigQuery, just a few

00:39:58.330 --> 00:40:00.700
concepts for those that
are unfamiliar in the room.

00:40:00.700 --> 00:40:03.715
So BigQuery has a
few constructs that

00:40:03.715 --> 00:40:05.840
are important to understand
before we go into this.

00:40:05.840 --> 00:40:07.540
The first is a slot,
which is basically

00:40:07.540 --> 00:40:10.790
just a construct of a fixed
amount of memory and CPU.

00:40:10.790 --> 00:40:11.890
You have a slot pool.

00:40:11.890 --> 00:40:14.050
So when you interact
with BigQuery,

00:40:14.050 --> 00:40:16.870
you have a series of slots
that you either use on-demand,

00:40:16.870 --> 00:40:18.940
or you have a fixed
reservation of these.

00:40:18.940 --> 00:40:21.970
In this case, Pandora
has 2,000 slots.

00:40:21.970 --> 00:40:23.470
They can make as
many reservations

00:40:23.470 --> 00:40:25.930
underneath that slot
pool as they would

00:40:25.930 --> 00:40:28.270
like for workload management.

00:40:28.270 --> 00:40:31.840
And this overall hierarchy
is called the reservation

00:40:31.840 --> 00:40:34.030
hierarchy, and
you see an example

00:40:34.030 --> 00:40:35.860
on the bottom right
of the screen.

00:40:35.860 --> 00:40:40.427
So workload management is
really here to enable SLAs,

00:40:40.427 --> 00:40:42.010
and this allows you
to really maximize

00:40:42.010 --> 00:40:43.410
your investment in BigQuery.

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:45.870
So what I mean by
maximizes is that what

00:40:45.870 --> 00:40:49.020
you see here is to
enable some SLAs,

00:40:49.020 --> 00:40:51.820
we're breaking apart
our slot reservation

00:40:51.820 --> 00:40:54.090
into batch and online.

00:40:54.090 --> 00:40:56.770
And we're giving particular
projects and particular

00:40:56.770 --> 00:41:02.050
applications a fixed amount
of computing and memory power.

00:41:02.050 --> 00:41:03.790
But if some of
these reservations--

00:41:03.790 --> 00:41:07.870
I mean, if some of these slots
are unused by these child

00:41:07.870 --> 00:41:11.740
projects, other projects can
actually burst in and use them.

00:41:11.740 --> 00:41:16.420
So if, for example,
the MADDS project

00:41:16.420 --> 00:41:18.940
is done with its
daily computation

00:41:18.940 --> 00:41:22.390
and is not using its slots,
Zeppelin users-- data

00:41:22.390 --> 00:41:25.120
scientists that are trying to
use the systems-- will benefit.

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:28.900
They can actually just go
over and take those slots.

00:41:28.900 --> 00:41:30.700
The next is federated query.

00:41:30.700 --> 00:41:34.330
So BigQuery traditionally
works on BigQuery storage.

00:41:34.330 --> 00:41:37.120
It's a highly optimized
both file format and storage

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:41.110
layer for BigQuery that really
enables true interactive query.

00:41:41.110 --> 00:41:42.760
But increasingly,
BigQuery supports

00:41:42.760 --> 00:41:45.100
more and more federated
query sources.

00:41:45.100 --> 00:41:49.022
So some of those include Google
Cloud Storage, Cloud SQL,

00:41:49.022 --> 00:41:49.730
and a few others.

00:41:49.730 --> 00:41:54.820
There's wonderful announcements
on that at Next 2019.

00:41:54.820 --> 00:41:58.700
So you can federate a query
across a variety of formats.

00:41:58.700 --> 00:42:01.740
So the formats that
Pandora primarily uses--

00:42:01.740 --> 00:42:08.170
Avro, Parquet, ORC-- the
majority of those formats

00:42:08.170 --> 00:42:09.910
are supported for
federated query.

00:42:09.910 --> 00:42:14.050
And BigQuery supports columnar
data partition support.

00:42:14.050 --> 00:42:17.320
So if you have data in
ORC or Parquet format,

00:42:17.320 --> 00:42:22.660
primarily ORC, BigQuery
will understand partitions

00:42:22.660 --> 00:42:25.630
and actually do predicate push
down and optimize the query,

00:42:25.630 --> 00:42:27.345
even if it's stored
external to BigQuery.

00:42:29.920 --> 00:42:31.930
What does federated
query enable?

00:42:31.930 --> 00:42:32.750
Storage tiering.

00:42:32.750 --> 00:42:34.270
So it's very common
for customers,

00:42:34.270 --> 00:42:36.910
and in Pandora's case, to
store their most hot data,

00:42:36.910 --> 00:42:40.000
their last 90 days,
in BigQuery and then

00:42:40.000 --> 00:42:42.940
push older or more
historical data into GCS,

00:42:42.940 --> 00:42:46.060
and then do federated query
across the two datasets

00:42:46.060 --> 00:42:48.540
when historical
analysis is required.

00:42:51.850 --> 00:42:54.970
The next is high throughput API
and external analysis engines.

00:42:54.970 --> 00:42:58.460
So we're innovating
with Pandora.

00:42:58.460 --> 00:43:01.720
The Google PSO team is working
to build a BigQuery storage

00:43:01.720 --> 00:43:06.580
handler that allows Hive
and Presto to register

00:43:06.580 --> 00:43:09.760
external tables against
BigQuery datasets,

00:43:09.760 --> 00:43:12.970
and this will allow for
further federation of queries.

00:43:12.970 --> 00:43:15.430
So data that is
historically preserved

00:43:15.430 --> 00:43:19.330
in GCS for multiple years
can be actually merged

00:43:19.330 --> 00:43:22.060
with the hot data in
BigQuery and queried

00:43:22.060 --> 00:43:24.700
with one analytical engine.

00:43:24.700 --> 00:43:28.450
And then lastly, exploration is
kind of taking place with BQML.

00:43:28.450 --> 00:43:31.270
So for simple regressions,
classifications,

00:43:31.270 --> 00:43:34.090
and increasingly
other ML methods,

00:43:34.090 --> 00:43:37.690
BQ is kind of making
it effortless to run

00:43:37.690 --> 00:43:42.300
those against datasets in BQ.

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:49.103
So some parting
anecdotes about what

00:43:49.103 --> 00:43:51.270
we were able to accomplish
over the last five months

00:43:51.270 --> 00:43:55.870
include an approach for GCP
speed disaster recovery.

00:43:55.870 --> 00:43:58.500
So like Brett was
mentioning, there's

00:43:58.500 --> 00:44:02.580
an active cluster in California
and a DR colo on the East

00:44:02.580 --> 00:44:06.390
Coast, and they're following
a similar model in GCP.

00:44:06.390 --> 00:44:09.690
So their primary
region is us-central1,

00:44:09.690 --> 00:44:13.990
with the DR region
being us-east1.

00:44:13.990 --> 00:44:16.410
And so there's a
couple of advantages

00:44:16.410 --> 00:44:21.890
of using the cloud
for DR. So the first

00:44:21.890 --> 00:44:25.830
is that you don't have to,
in an active standby mode,

00:44:25.830 --> 00:44:29.340
have resources be running
in your disaster recovery.

00:44:29.340 --> 00:44:32.520
And if you needed to
actually replicate

00:44:32.520 --> 00:44:34.890
your entire production
environment in the cloud,

00:44:34.890 --> 00:44:37.930
that's possible by moving
to a second region.

00:44:37.930 --> 00:44:40.470
But there was one
really neat thing

00:44:40.470 --> 00:44:43.590
that Pandora was able to
adopt within GCS that is going

00:44:43.590 --> 00:44:45.520
to make this quite effortless.

00:44:45.520 --> 00:44:48.695
So GCS has a bunch of
storage tiering modes.

00:44:48.695 --> 00:44:50.070
So for those that
are unfamiliar,

00:44:50.070 --> 00:44:55.000
there's multi-region GCS
storage, regional GCS storage,

00:44:55.000 --> 00:44:56.730
and a special type
of multi-region

00:44:56.730 --> 00:44:58.770
is called dual region.

00:44:58.770 --> 00:45:01.080
What this does is pin your
data between two regions

00:45:01.080 --> 00:45:02.740
that are known.

00:45:02.740 --> 00:45:07.750
So that's especially useful in
disaster recovery scenarios,

00:45:07.750 --> 00:45:08.990
amongst others.

00:45:08.990 --> 00:45:11.500
And so this NAM4
bucket type within GCS

00:45:11.500 --> 00:45:14.470
is used by Pandora to store
its most important data

00:45:14.470 --> 00:45:17.680
and ensure that it's replicated
from us-central1 and us-east1

00:45:17.680 --> 00:45:21.670
continuously, and allow
them to spin up and continue

00:45:21.670 --> 00:45:24.700
their business in the event
of a disaster in us-central1.

00:45:24.700 --> 00:45:28.120
And they can do that via CI/CD
tools, and infrastructure,

00:45:28.120 --> 00:45:31.180
and code that they've developed
throughout this project,

00:45:31.180 --> 00:45:33.790
as well.

00:45:33.790 --> 00:45:37.440
And then lastly, kind of ending
with a really early success

00:45:37.440 --> 00:45:40.860
story for some of the
other tools in GCP.

00:45:40.860 --> 00:45:42.900
So there's the Pandora
Music Genome Project,

00:45:42.900 --> 00:45:47.790
so I encourage everyone to go to
pandora.com/about/mgp and learn

00:45:47.790 --> 00:45:49.720
a little bit about this.

00:45:49.720 --> 00:45:55.870
It's a very comprehensive
analysis of music over time.

00:45:55.870 --> 00:46:01.050
And what this is is
batch feature extraction

00:46:01.050 --> 00:46:03.390
and prediction using TensorFlow.

00:46:03.390 --> 00:46:05.850
And so traditionally
at Pandora, this

00:46:05.850 --> 00:46:09.900
was done on a single server
using other ML libraries

00:46:09.900 --> 00:46:12.720
and it would take
a matter of days.

00:46:12.720 --> 00:46:15.180
In the example
here, over six days.

00:46:15.180 --> 00:46:19.050
So analyzing over
10,000 MP3s, extracting

00:46:19.050 --> 00:46:23.130
features for analysis was
just done very, very slowly.

00:46:23.130 --> 00:46:25.650
And they were able to quickly
port that over to Dataflow,

00:46:25.650 --> 00:46:28.420
after this data was
loaded into GCS,

00:46:28.420 --> 00:46:32.310
and bring up like 800 cores
and complete this same effort

00:46:32.310 --> 00:46:34.290
in less than four hours.

00:46:34.290 --> 00:46:38.838
And that's just the start of
what they're doing with this.

00:46:38.838 --> 00:46:40.630
And so with this, I'm
going to pass it over

00:46:40.630 --> 00:46:42.463
to Brett to talk about
what Pandora is going

00:46:42.463 --> 00:46:44.260
to be doing for the
rest of 2019 and what

00:46:44.260 --> 00:46:49.082
we kind of hope to see them
talk about at Next next year.

00:46:49.082 --> 00:46:50.994
BRETT UYESHIRO: Thanks, Blake.

00:46:50.994 --> 00:46:54.340
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:57.700 --> 00:47:00.370
So just try to finish
off real quick so we have

00:47:00.370 --> 00:47:01.570
time for a couple questions.

00:47:04.190 --> 00:47:08.290
We are part of the way down
a pretty long journey here,

00:47:08.290 --> 00:47:11.140
but some things we're
already realizing

00:47:11.140 --> 00:47:15.800
is the separation of
storage and compute

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:19.870
is one of the biggest wins that
we're getting out of the move

00:47:19.870 --> 00:47:21.100
to a cloud.

00:47:21.100 --> 00:47:25.510
Enabling this
flexibility to scale out

00:47:25.510 --> 00:47:29.450
when you really need to,
when you have a bottleneck,

00:47:29.450 --> 00:47:31.330
has been really cool.

00:47:31.330 --> 00:47:34.990
As an example, we
have this job that

00:47:34.990 --> 00:47:39.200
processes-- it's essentially ETL
on a lot of our listener data.

00:47:39.200 --> 00:47:42.010
It has probably the
worst name possible.

00:47:42.010 --> 00:47:44.230
It's called the Nightly Job.

00:47:44.230 --> 00:47:48.430
It was the only job back
in 2010 when we wrote it,

00:47:48.430 --> 00:47:51.850
but it still does a lot of the
core processing of our listener

00:47:51.850 --> 00:47:53.470
data.

00:47:53.470 --> 00:47:58.360
That used to run, or currently
runs, on-prem in just a little

00:47:58.360 --> 00:48:00.400
over five hours.

00:48:00.400 --> 00:48:02.230
We moved that over to GCP.

00:48:02.230 --> 00:48:05.950
It's running in Dataproc
now, and it runs in, I think,

00:48:05.950 --> 00:48:07.070
an hour and a half.

00:48:07.070 --> 00:48:08.930
So that was a big bottleneck.

00:48:08.930 --> 00:48:11.560
It's a big dependency for
this long chain of jobs

00:48:11.560 --> 00:48:12.860
that we run every night.

00:48:12.860 --> 00:48:15.970
And so being able to scale
that out, reduce the time,

00:48:15.970 --> 00:48:17.680
puts us in a good
position to finish

00:48:17.680 --> 00:48:19.210
all of the other
nightly processing

00:48:19.210 --> 00:48:20.530
and be ready for the next day.

00:48:24.070 --> 00:48:28.357
The operational
overhead-- well, let

00:48:28.357 --> 00:48:30.190
me talk about
Lift-and-improve a little bit.

00:48:30.190 --> 00:48:33.370
So being able to
migrate, we didn't

00:48:33.370 --> 00:48:36.010
want to just lift and shift,
take exactly what we were doing

00:48:36.010 --> 00:48:38.650
on-prem and move it to a cloud.

00:48:38.650 --> 00:48:43.420
So all these things I mentioned
about being able to scale out

00:48:43.420 --> 00:48:46.840
have really helped us there.

00:48:46.840 --> 00:48:49.810
The ability-- a
Hive upgrade used

00:48:49.810 --> 00:48:52.110
to be a really big
endeavor for us.

00:48:52.110 --> 00:48:55.060
It took us about
a year from start

00:48:55.060 --> 00:48:58.620
to finish to test
out the major Hive

00:48:58.620 --> 00:49:03.940
version to get all of
our different teams

00:49:03.940 --> 00:49:08.110
that have tested the
job and do that upgrade.

00:49:08.110 --> 00:49:10.780
And now with Dataproc, and
having ephemeral clusters,

00:49:10.780 --> 00:49:14.350
and having jobs being able
to use whatever version they

00:49:14.350 --> 00:49:18.160
need to, we can do this in
a much more staged fashion.

00:49:18.160 --> 00:49:22.210
It gives us a lot more
control and flexibility.

00:49:22.210 --> 00:49:25.600
And the last thing I would
say is, lean on Google.

00:49:25.600 --> 00:49:27.940
Lean on the PSO services.

00:49:27.940 --> 00:49:29.930
They've been a great
partner for us.

00:49:29.930 --> 00:49:31.720
A big migration like this--

00:49:31.720 --> 00:49:34.510
things do not always go right.

00:49:34.510 --> 00:49:37.160
Features aren't
always as advertised.

00:49:37.160 --> 00:49:38.230
That's just the fact.

00:49:38.230 --> 00:49:40.430
People use them
in different ways.

00:49:40.430 --> 00:49:42.280
But Google has been
a great partner.

00:49:42.280 --> 00:49:44.530
When we found an
issue, we reported it.

00:49:44.530 --> 00:49:46.240
They get back to
us very quickly,

00:49:46.240 --> 00:49:47.620
and we're iterating on it.

00:49:47.620 --> 00:49:49.655
So we really appreciate that.

00:49:49.655 --> 00:49:51.530
We know everything's
not going to be perfect,

00:49:51.530 --> 00:49:52.930
but when you have
a good partner,

00:49:52.930 --> 00:49:55.910
it makes a big difference.

00:49:55.910 --> 00:49:58.690
Also, early access to features--

00:49:58.690 --> 00:50:03.400
we got early access to Data
Catalog, which has been great,

00:50:03.400 --> 00:50:07.360
a lot of BigQuery features
that we got access to early,

00:50:07.360 --> 00:50:10.160
so that was really helpful.

00:50:10.160 --> 00:50:11.150
So what's next for us?

00:50:11.150 --> 00:50:14.140
We're really focusing
on optimization.

00:50:14.140 --> 00:50:16.270
So we're going to
finish the migration

00:50:16.270 --> 00:50:17.720
beginning of next year.

00:50:17.720 --> 00:50:20.680
Along the way, we're doing a
lot of optimization for cost,

00:50:20.680 --> 00:50:23.560
for speed, and then
really digging in

00:50:23.560 --> 00:50:26.260
to see what we can
leverage with all the tools

00:50:26.260 --> 00:50:30.367
to improve the personalization
and listening experience

00:50:30.367 --> 00:50:31.450
for all of you on Pandora.

00:50:31.450 --> 00:50:34.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

